WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.250
Hey, I’m Dean Peterson, a filmmaker from
Brooklyn, NY.&nbsp;

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:07.320
I made the film INCREDIBLY SMALL and I’m
about to shoot my second feature film WHAT

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.320
CHILDREN DO.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.330
When we shot my first film, we shot it on
the SONY EX1 camera and we used old Nikon

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:20.369
prime lenses with a Letus adapter, which gave
it a really great image.&nbsp;

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:26.640
The old Nikon lenses mixed with the spinning
glass and the Letus adapter gave it this smooth,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.230
creamy, filmic feel, which we really liked.&nbsp;

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:40.190
We chose that camera partly because my DP
owned it, but also partly because back in

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:46.490
2009 when we shot the movie, DSLR cameras
were not really a mainstay of the industry

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:47.490
yet.&nbsp;

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:52.940
The Canon 5D hadn’t even been out for a
year and films like TINY FURNITURE, which

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.490
opened the door for using these DSLRS, hadn’t
even come out yet.&nbsp;

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.880
So we had limited options.&nbsp;

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:07.500
Back then people were still using the traditional
camcorders like the HVX’s a lot.&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.460
We just used what we had.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:14.679
The change we made from the first movie until
we made the second is incredible in terms

00:01:14.679 --> 00:01:15.679
of cameras.&nbsp;

00:01:15.679 --> 00:01:22.889
Now you can go to Best Buy and purchase a
$700 DSLR that shoots 4K and you can shoot

00:01:22.889 --> 00:01:27.409
in pitch black, whereas back then is simply
wasn’t the case.&nbsp;

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:29.280
There was still a lot of limitations.&nbsp;

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:34.459
It was a completely different landscape back
then (2009 and prior).

00:01:34.459 --> 00:01:39.701
The decision of what were going to shoot on
this time was a lot more involved and we had

00:01:39.701 --> 00:01:42.520
a lot more options and research to do.&nbsp;

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:47.399
We talked about what we wanted the image to
look like for this film and we also talked

00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:48.490
about process.&nbsp;

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:54.920
We are going to be a very small, nimble crew
and we’re going to be shooting very quickly

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.979
and we didn’t want to deal with a lot of
peripheral accessories that you needed to

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:00.979
add on to the camera.&nbsp;

00:02:00.979 --> 00:02:03.869
We didn’t want a camera that necessitated
a huge camera department.&nbsp;

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:10.530
We wanted to be as agile and adaptable as
we possibly could.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:17.420
My DP Darren and I had shot a film on the
Black Magic (cinema camera) and we really

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.560
liked how that looked.&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:27.970
We liked that the Pocket cinema camera is
small and looks great, but you have to add

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.510
all this stuff to the camera.&nbsp;

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:31.700
The battery kind of “sucks.”&nbsp;

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.500
You have to have an external power source.&nbsp;

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:39.012
It’s hard to do hand-held stuff with such
a small camera, so you end up putting it in

00:02:39.012 --> 00:02:40.860
a cage and a shoulder rig.&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.310
So by the end of adding all this stuff and
complications, it’s kind of as big and as

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:51.380
expensive as all these other cameras.

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.130
The camera that we finally decided to shoot
on was the Canon C300.&nbsp;

00:02:55.130 --> 00:03:03.150
I shot a music video on it before and I loved
the way it looks.&nbsp;

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:10.010
I feel like it takes all of the barriers out
of the way when you’re shooting.&nbsp;

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:16.300
I feel like it’s easy to get a great image
quality out of it.&nbsp;

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.650
The ‘Low-Light’ is incredible.&nbsp;

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:23.930
You can go into locations and not need to
light them too much, which is a big part of

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:30.100
how we are going to shoot this movie – we
are going to go into a lot of rooms and businesses

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:34.480
and try to use as much natural/available light
as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.740
The Canon C300 is the best kind of camera
I’ve ever used for that kind of shooting.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:45.760
I do find myself going online and falling
to the hysteria of camera culture that exists

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:46.760
on there.&nbsp;

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:53.610
And it’s always these camera comparison
tests of like guys shooting the side of their

00:03:53.610 --> 00:04:01.990
house or the blowing of leaves in their back
yard to show the muddled compression of one

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:03.480
camera versus another.&nbsp;

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:11.319
At that point, I find it good to step back
and&nbsp; just remind yourself that at the end

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:15.130
of the day we’re supposed to be storytellers.&nbsp;

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:20.280
These cameras, tools and technology that we
use are just supposed to be there in service

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.410
of telling a story and not as a cool toy to
have.&nbsp;

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:33.840
Even thought I do find it shockingly easy
to get swept up in the debate of how many

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:40.600
stops of dynamic range something has, what
is the best codec, what bitrates this kind

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:48.340
of camera has, it is always best to remind
myself that at the end of the day these are

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:49.630
not the most important things.&nbsp;

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:53.870
It is definitely important, but it’s not
the most important consideration when you’re

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:54.280
making a movie.

